### Type-Safe Redux-Style Actions and Exhaustive Reducers

The foundation is a discriminated union of action types. Each variant's `type` field is a distinct string literal, and its `payload` field type depends on which variant it is. Build that union from a single source-of-truth map instead of writing each action interface by hand. That is what keeps action creators and the reducer's payload types automatically in sync.

```typescript
interface ActionPayloads {
  increment: { amount: number };
  setUser: { id: string; name: string };
  reset: undefined;
}

// Build the discriminated union once from the payload map
type Action = {
  [K in keyof ActionPayloads]: ActionPayloads[K] extends undefined
    ? { type: K }
    : { type: K; payload: ActionPayloads[K] };
}[keyof ActionPayloads];

// Action creators stay generic over the action's own type, so each one returns
// the exact variant, not the wide Action union
function createAction<K extends keyof ActionPayloads>(
  type: K,
  ...args: ActionPayloads[K] extends undefined ? [] : [payload: ActionPayloads[K]]
): Action {
  return args.length ? { type, payload: args[0] } as Action : { type } as Action;
}

const inc = createAction('increment', { amount: 5 });
// { type: 'increment'; payload: { amount: number } }

const reset = createAction('reset');
// { type: 'reset' }
// createAction('increment'); // error: expected 1 argument, payload is required here

interface State { count: number; user: { id: string; name: string } | null; }

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'increment':
      // payload narrowed automatically
      return { ...state, count: state.count + action.payload.amount };
    case 'setUser':
      return { ...state, user: action.payload };
    case 'reset':
      return { ...state, count: 0, user: null };
    default:
      const _exhaustive: never = action; // compile error if a variant is left unhandled
      return state;
  }
}
```

The mapped type building `Action` is what removes the duplication. Adding one entry to `ActionPayloads` automatically produces three things. A new union member, a new accepted `createAction` call shape, and a new case the reducer's `switch` must handle. No hand-written interfaces to touch.

Inside each `case`, narrowing on `action.type` narrows `action.payload` too. That works because `Action` is a proper discriminated union keyed on the literal `type` field.

The `default: const _exhaustive: never = action;` line is the exhaustiveness check. Say a developer adds `'decrement'` to `ActionPayloads` but forgets the matching `case`. Then `action`'s type inside `default` is no longer narrowed all the way to `never`, because a variant remains unhandled. Assigning it to a `never`-typed variable becomes a compile error, pointing exactly at the missing case.

Common pitfall: forgetting that the conditional `args` tuple type only works when `K` is a single concrete literal at the call site. If `K` is left as the full `keyof ActionPayloads` union somewhere upstream, that conditional collapses to a union of tuple shapes. TypeScript cannot cleanly use such a union to validate call arguments, and the strictness the pattern was meant to provide quietly disappears.

